CAT = "capswComm"; SEG = "COM"
# VLO 5 (therapeutic relationships, boundaries, privacy) and VLO 6 (observation, reporting, documentation).
Q = [
("Which is an objective observation?",
 "Skin on the left heel is red", ["Client says her heel hurts", "Client feels very tired today", "Client says he slept badly"],
 "Objective observations are what you see, hear, smell or measure yourself. What the client tells you, such as pain or tiredness, is subjective and is recorded in their own words."),
("A client says, \"My stomach is killing me.\" How should you record it?",
 "Quote the client's own words", ["Write that the client has gas", "Record it as indigestion", "Leave it out as subjective"],
 "Subjective information is recorded as the client said it, in quotation marks, then reported. Writing \"gas\" or \"indigestion\" is a diagnosis, which is outside the PSW role."),
("Which change should you report to the nurse right away?",
 "New confusion in a client", ["A client wanting tea, not coffee", "A client asking for the radio", "A client choosing a blue shirt"],
 "A sudden change in mental state can signal infection, low blood sugar, a stroke or other problems and must be reported at once. Preferences are respected and noted but are not urgent."),
("You made an error in a paper chart. How should you correct it?",
 "One line through it, initial it", ["Cover it with correction fluid", "Scribble over it until it is hidden", "Tear out the page and redo it"],
 "Draw a single line through the error so it stays readable, then initial and date it as policy requires. Hiding or removing entries makes the record unreliable and may be seen as falsification."),
("When should care be documented?",
 "After it is given", ["Before you give it", "At the end of the week", "Only if something goes wrong"],
 "Chart care after you give it, as soon as practical. Charting in advance records care that may never happen, and leaving it until later makes the record inaccurate."),
("Which is an open-ended question?",
 "How did you sleep last night?", ["Did you sleep well last night?", "Are you in pain right now?", "Do you want the window shut?"],
 "Open-ended questions invite the client to talk and cannot be answered with yes or no. The others are closed questions, useful for quick facts."),
("A client tells you she is scared of her surgery tomorrow. Which reply is most therapeutic?",
 "Tell me what worries you most", ["Don't worry, it will go fine", "Everyone feels that way", "Let's talk about something nice"],
 "Inviting her to share her fears is active listening. False reassurance, generalizing and changing the subject close the conversation and leave her feelings unheard."),
("A client has expressive aphasia after a stroke. What does this mean?",
 "Trouble putting thoughts into words", ["Trouble hearing what is said", "Trouble seeing the speaker's face", "Trouble remembering events from long ago"],
 "Expressive aphasia affects producing speech or writing, though the person often understands. Give extra time, use yes or no questions, and offer picture boards or gestures."),
("How should you speak to a client who is hard of hearing?",
 "Face them and speak clearly", ["Shout into the better ear", "Speak quickly and keep it brief", "Talk while walking behind"],
 "Facing the client in good light lets them lip-read and see your expressions. Speak clearly in a lower pitch; shouting distorts words."),
("How can you help a client with low vision find food on the plate?",
 "Describe it by clock positions", ["Move the food as they eat", "Feed them every meal yourself", "Use a plate the same colour as food"],
 "Describing food by clock face (\"potatoes at 3 o'clock\") supports independence. A plate that contrasts with the food also helps."),
("Which Ontario law protects the privacy of clients' personal health information?",
 "PHIPA", ["FIPPA", "PIPEDA", "OHSA"],
 "The Personal Health Information Protection Act, 2004 (PHIPA) governs how health information custodians and their agents, including PSWs, collect, use and share health information. PIPEDA is the federal private-sector privacy law."),
("A neighbour asks how your client is doing after a hospital stay. What should you say?",
 "That you cannot share that", ["That she is doing much better", "Only her diagnosis, not details", "To ask the client's doctor"],
 "Under PHIPA, health information is shared only with those in the circle of care or with consent. Even a general update about a client's health is a privacy breach."),
("You realize you left a client's chart open on a public computer. What should you do?",
 "Close it and report the breach", ["Close it and say nothing about it", "Leave it for the next nurse", "Wait to see if anyone used it"],
 "Secure the information right away and report the possible privacy breach to your supervisor, who follows PHIPA breach procedures. Hiding it prevents the custodian from meeting its duties."),
("Which posting on social media is acceptable for a PSW?",
 "A general post about loving the job", ["A selfie with a smiling resident", "A funny story about a client", "A photo of the home's dining room at lunch"],
 "Posting photos or stories about clients, even without names, can identify them and breaks privacy and boundaries. Talking about the job in general terms is fine."),
("A client offers you $50 for being so kind. What should you do?",
 "Thank them and politely decline", ["Accept it so they are not hurt", "Accept it and share it with staff", "Take it but tell nobody"],
 "Most employers forbid accepting money or gifts because it blurs professional boundaries and can lead to exploitation. Decline kindly and follow policy if the client insists."),
("Which is a sign that a professional boundary is being crossed?",
 "Visiting a client on your day off", ["Calling a client by preferred name", "Listening to a client's stories", "Smiling during morning care"],
 "Spending personal time with a client turns a therapeutic relationship into a social one, which risks favouritism and exploitation. Using a preferred name and listening are good practice."),
("What should a verbal report to the nurse include?",
 "What you saw, when, and what you did", ["Your guess about the diagnosis", "What the family thinks probably caused it", "Only problems you could not fix"],
 "A useful report is factual: the observation, the time, what the client said and what action you took. Guessing at a diagnosis is outside the PSW role."),
("Which time is written correctly in the 24-hour clock for 3:30 p.m.?",
 "1530", ["0330", "1330", "1503"],
 "In the 24-hour clock, add 12 to afternoon hours: 3:30 p.m. is 1530. 0330 is 3:30 a.m."),
("Which barrier to communication is the PSW causing here: \"You shouldn't feel that way\"?",
 "Judging feelings", ["Using jargon", "Open questions", "Silence"],
 "Telling a client how they should feel dismisses and judges their emotions. Accepting feelings without judgement keeps the conversation open."),
("Why is touch used carefully in communication?",
 "Its meaning varies by culture", ["It is never allowed during personal care", "It always calms upset clients", "It replaces the need to talk"],
 "Touch can comfort, but people and cultures differ in how they feel about it. Ask, watch the client's response and respect their wishes."),
("Which client observation should be reported because it may signal infection?",
 "Cloudy, smelly urine", ["Pale yellow, clear urine", "The usual amount of urine", "Urine with no odour"],
 "Cloudy, foul-smelling urine can mean a urinary tract infection, which in older adults may also show as new confusion. Clear pale urine is normal."),
("What is the main purpose of a shift report between PSWs and nurses?",
 "To keep care safe", ["To rate speed", "To plan overtime", "To share gossip"],
 "Shift report passes on changes, concerns and care still needed so the next shift can continue safe care. It is not a place for gossip."),
("A client who speaks little English needs to give consent for care. What is the best support?",
 "A trained interpreter", ["Their 10-year-old grandchild", "Loud, slow English", "Nodding until they agree"],
 "A trained interpreter gives accurate, confidential communication. Children may misunderstand, and nodding or speaking loudly does not confirm understanding."),
("What does active listening involve?",
 "Attention and checking meaning", ["Planning your reply while they talk", "Finishing the client's sentences", "Giving advice as soon as possible"],
 "Active listening means full attention, body language that shows interest, and reflecting or paraphrasing to check you understood. Interrupting or advising too soon shuts the client down."),
]
